  You missed the turn deadline. The computer ordered Defend actions for your
generals. If we receive a late move, we will process it and send you the turn
results. If you miss 2 turns, the computer will drop you from the game.





  Quote of the Week:

  Saint Ignatius Loyola, Spanish soldier and founder of the Jesuit Order, 
1522:
  "To arrive at the truth in all things, we ought always to be ready to 
believe that what seems to us white, is black if the hierarchical Church so 
defines it."


ACTION REPORT    

Here's what happened to Your Majesty this turn:

    Xin II was visited by a hero from Valhalla, named Kelderek!    The  
characteristics for this great King from Game 159 are given in your 
"CHARACTERS" report.
  If Kelderek searches a feature, any monetary treasure Kelderek captures 
will be given to you.  If Kelderek captures a magic treasure, Kelderek will 
give it to your King when he departs for Valhalla, or you can order Kelderek 
to Give it to one of your characters.  You can do anything you want with the 
magic item Kelderek came with, but the item will leave at the end of his 
stay with you.  Except for the rules given in this paragraph, Kelderek can 
generally be treated just like your other Heroes, and can be affected by any 
spell except Resurrection.  The main limitation in using Kelderek is that 
Kelderek has only enough patience to practice with you for one of the 
phase(s) Kelderek is with you.  Kelderek will leave at the end of the last 
phase Kelderek is scheduled to serve you, after all battles have been 
fought.  Kelderek can lead armies, but when Kelderek returns to Valhalla, 
the armies will normally be left leaderless in the province they are in.  If 
Kelderek has the armies in a province you don't own, they will be 
transferred to your lowest-numbered leader in the same province, or at last 
resort, the garrison commander in an adjacent province owned by you.  If no 
leader is available, the armies will be disbanded.
  Kelderek can be given orders only for the first 2 phase(s) of the turn.

  Thus endeth the adventures of Xin II for this year.



  Here is the financial report for Xin II's kingdom: this turn, the Kingdom 
collected 0 gold in revenue.  0 gold was expended to finance our leaders.  0 
gold was expended to maintain our armies.  Xin II was left with 3 gold in 
the treasury.


LETTER REPORTS

  O King of Kings, thy heroes have travelled around the globe in
search of adventure and conquest.  I have faithfully kept their
correspondence, and now I present it to thee:


From: Moosu, Squire to Belleus
To: Xin II

  Honored Sovereign, 

  Frigid winter melted into spring, and Belleus prepared to carry out his 
urgent orders.  He prepared his armies to defend Hong Kong and the 
surrounding provinces.

  Thy humble servant,   Moosu, Squire to Belleus


From: Gordius
To: Xin II

  Honored Sovereign, 

  In spring, I prepared my armies to defend Ulan Bator and the surrounding 
provinces.

  Thy humble servant,   Gordius


